 "_comment": "Minimal version of the article's biography experiment (unpublished there): N individuals x 4 attributes x 32 values, 2-layer transformer (d = 128, 4 heads), online training, batch 128, Adam 1e-3. Each example: 10 random distractor tokens with a two-token name (unique pairs of 256 + 256 name parts) at a random position, then the attribute token; the value is predicted at the last position. Evaluation: a fixed subset of 4,096 facts. Article claims: plateau length grows ~linearly with N; growing RBF and wide Cos-Bochner FFNs shorten it drastically; GeGLU plateaus at a lower ceiling. rbf_last_*: the last FFN is a growing RBF layer (temperature 1, value rate eta), the first is GeGLU (width 512). *_ft: after the main phase, 4,000 steps on 1,024 new individuals only (no replay); the article reports strong forgetting for parametric FFNs and that RCDL layers can 'unforget' without replay. The earlier two-token task (name, attribute) had no plateau at N = 1024 (all facts learned within 500 steps), see rcdl/facts.py.",
